.TH TKSCENETEST 1
.SH NAME
tkscenetest \- check that libtk's render-node path draws what the direct path draws
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tkscenetest
.RB [ -v ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tkscenetest
renders each of a set of cases twice \(em once through
.IR libtk 's
direct path, which paints into the destination image, and once through its
render-node path, which describes the widget as a tree of nodes and renders
the tree \(em and checks that the two produce the same pixels, byte for
byte.
.PP
The cases are one of every widget kind with default options, and then the
widget
.I states
that have node kinds of their own: a selected entry, an entry with
.BR -show ,
a disabled button, a canvas with a rectangle, a line, an oval, a string and
a filled polygon in it, a listbox with a selection, a text widget with
content, a scale away from its minimum, a part-scrolled scrollbar, a
non-default font, a sunken frame, a focused entry, a label clipped by the
image it is rendered into, non-default colours by name, a label with a
.B -bitmap
and a posted menu holding a separator and a cascade. Default options alone
would pass while every one of those was broken.
.PP
Three of those are there for a node kind nothing else reaches.
.B TkNpolyfill
is the cascade arrow's node and is otherwise seen only in a posted menu, so
the canvas grows a filled polygon as well.
.B TkNtexture
names a
.I file
rather than carrying pixels \(em which is why an image that arrived as a
file descriptor is the one thing the vocabulary cannot describe \(em so it
needs a real path, and a bare
.B -bitmap
name resolves to
.BI /icons/tk/ name
rather than being taken as absolute. And the posted menu's separator paints
into the window itself, which makes the walk
.I flush
in the middle of the menu: the case that says the menu's drawing code must
not hold the walk's tree across it, because the flush frees it.
.PP
That identity is the whole acceptance condition for the node tree: a
description that draws something other than what the direct path draws is
not a description of the drawing.
.PP
The path is selected with the
.B render
widget command,
.B direct
or
.BR scene .
.B Direct
is the default, because
.B tkviascene
is zero-initialised, so nothing in a running system takes the node path
unless it is asked for.
.PP
.B "render scaled ?num ?den??"
sets the scale the walk renders its trees at, and with no argument
reports it along with what the last scaled renders could not be exact
for. The scale is a rational because the renderer has no floating point
\(em it has to work in a kernel \(em and positions scale about the
target's top left, so that corner stays put and the tree grows into it.
This is
.B "resolution independence:"
the same nodes drawn larger, which is the one thing a description can do
that a raster cannot.
.PP
Geometry scales exactly. Two node kinds cannot and are
.B counted
rather than hidden:
.B TkNglyphs
names a font
.I file
whose size is in its name, so text keeps its natural size until a
size\-selection step exists, and
.BR TkNtexture 's
image is drawn by
.IR draw (2),
which clips and replicates but does not resample. What this test checks,
with the same figures on the host and on the Pi 3B kernel:
.IP
.EX
scale 1:1              1 1 nodes 0 approx 0
a raised frame at 2:1  2 1 nodes 4 approx 0
a label at 2:1         2 1 nodes 4 approx 1
.EE
.PP
So
.B "approx 0"
means something, because
.B "approx 1"
is demonstrated beside it: a frame is pure geometry and enlarges
exactly, a label carries a glyph run and does not. The 2:1 render is
checked by difference from the 1:1 one (2040 bytes) rather than by a
byte comparison, because nothing can produce a 2x render to compare it
against \(em that is the point of it. Setting the scale back to 1:1 must
then be byte\-identical, which a scale that leaked state would fail
after passing everything else.
.PP
With
.B -v
each passing check is printed as well as each failing one.
.PP
Every comparison is a readback from an image allocated for the purpose,
never from the screen, for the reason
.IR tkofftest (1)
gives: what a widget put on the screen cannot be read back, while an
allocated image can. That is also what lets this run on a kernel with no
display attached \(em it takes a
.B Display
from its
.B Context
when it has one and allocates a bare one when it does not \(em which is the
case it exists for.
.SH "THE TWO CONTROLS, AND WHY A PIXEL COMPARISON NEEDS THEM"
A zero from a predicate is worth nothing until the predicate is shown able
to fire, and an identity check has two distinct ways of passing while
testing nothing.
.TP
.B "the node path never ran"
Identity between the two rounds is exactly what you get if
.B "render scene"
silently did nothing and both rounds took the direct path. So
.B "render count"
is read back and its three figures \(em trees, widgets, nodes \(em must all
be non-zero. A zero there is a failure even when every image matches.
.TP
.B "the comparison cannot see a difference"
A comparison that always reported equality would make every identity check
pass. So two
.I different
widgets are compared as well, and must differ.
.PP
A case may be
.BR skipped ,
counted separately from a pass and a failure, when the environment cannot
distinguish it from the case it is meant to differ from \(em the only such
case is the non-default font, and the Pi 3B kernel's root carries exactly
one font, which is also Tk's default. A case that is dropped is reported,
because coverage quietly lost is worse than coverage never claimed.
.PP
Two further properties of the description are checked, because a tree that
draws correctly can still be useless to a backend: every node that marks
pixels must describe its own box, and the union the trees marked must be a
non-empty rectangle inside the image.
.SH "THE WALK'S OVERFLOW FALLBACK"
.B tkwalkopen()
pushes one walk level per window drawn, and a canvas window item is a
window, so a nest of canvases is a nest of walk levels.
.B Maxwalk
is 8 \(em commented "a canvas in a text in a canvas ..." \(em and past it
.B tkwalkopen
returns without pushing: "deeper than this: no batching, still correct".
.IR tkofftest (1)
nests a canvas, a frame and an entry, which is two or three levels, so
nothing reached that fallback until this program built a nest ten deep to
force it.
.PP
.B "That the count CAPS rather than being a constant needs three depths."
With only a shallow and a deep measurement, a tree count that happened
always to be 8 would satisfy "capped at
.BR Maxwalk ".
Measured:
.PP
.EX
depth  2 -> 3 trees   (unsaturated 3)
depth  5 -> 6 trees   (unsaturated 6)
depth 10 -> 8 trees   (unsaturated 11, Maxwalk 8)
.EE
.PP
so it scales with depth below the cap and stops at it above, and the claim
under test \(em "still correct" \(em holds: the nest past the cap renders
identically through both paths.
.PP
The first version of this check asked whether
.I batching
was lost, on the reasoning that widgets-per-tree would fall. That was wrong
about the mechanism:
.B tkwalkopen
flushes the enclosing walk's pending tree before pushing, so a nested
window always gets a tree of its own and the ratio is 1.00 at every depth.
There is no batching here to lose. The tree count saturating is what says
the fallback fired.
.SH "ONE READ PER ROUND"
.BR "render count" ,
.B "render nodes"
and
.B "render bounds"
share one set of accumulators and
.I each read clears all of them
.RI ( tkwalkreset (\^)
in
.BR libtk/utils.c ).
Reading two in a row gives the second one zero. This program therefore
renders a fresh round before each read, and anything else that asks for
more than one of those figures must do the same.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/tkscenetest.b
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR tkofftest (1),
.IR tkcmd (1)
.SH BUGS
What it does
.B not
cover:
.PP
The widgets are laid out with
.B pack
into a 160\(mu60 image. The four states this once listed as uncovered \(em a
focused widget, a partially-clipped one, a posted menu with a cascade and a
non-default colour scheme \(em are now covered. A widget inside a
.B canvas
window item is covered too, and that entry was wrong when it was written:
the
.B Maxwalk
nest below is built from
.B "create window \(em window"
at every level, so a widget in a canvas window item is exercised at depths
one through ten rather than not at all.
.PP
.B "RESOLVED: the population is now all seventeen widget types,"
.B "and that claim is checkable from the program's own output."
This paragraph used to say every widget kind was covered. It was not:
.B "render types"
showed
.B panel
and
.B choicebutton
absent from the population entirely \(em the test had no opinion on
them \(em and
.B separator
present but reading
.BR "separator 2 0" ,
two draws and no joins, because the gate in
.I tkdrawframe
named
.B TKframe
alone. An assertion of coverage that does not state its population is
not one a reader can check, which is how it stood unexamined.
.PP
The line now reads, for all seventeen:
.IP
.EX
frame 38 38 label 12 12 checkbutton 1 1 button 3 3
menubutton 1 1 menu 2 2 separator 2 2 cascade 2 2
listbox 4 4 scrollbar 2 2 text 2 2 canvas 4 0
entry 4 4 radiobutton 1 1 scale 2 2 panel 1 0
choicebutton 1 1
.EE
.PP
Two join nothing, both deliberately.
.B Canvas
builds its own scene per draw and frees it, so its items never enter the
shared walk.
.B Panel
is the one widget whose content is the application's own live image,
handed in by
.BR putimage :
.B TkNtexture
names a
.BR file ,
which is what keeps a tree free of display-side resources so it can
outlive the repaint that built it, and a panel's image has no path. So
.I tkdrawpanel
calls
.I tkwalkflush
and paints directly, and a tree cannot describe a panel without giving
up the property that makes it worth having. A GPU backend would want a
texture
.I handle
here rather than a path, which is the one place that design would need
extending.
.PP
What is left, so far as this program can tell, is the things it says in
.B "Where it has actually been run"
rather than a widget kind or a state. If something is missing it is
something neither this nor
.IR tkofftest (1)
thought to name, which is the kind of gap a list cannot close.
.PP
.B "RESOLVED: a listbox's -width and -height are PIXELS here, not characters."
The scrolled case used to skip, and the cause was in this program rather
than in
.IR libtk .
A listbox or text widget given
.B "-width 12 -height 3"
comes out
.B 14\(mu5
pixels, because those options are a size and not a character count \(em
.B "-width 120 -height 44"
gives 122\(mu46. So four cases were drawing a five-pixel sliver, less than
one line tall, and nothing could scroll because nothing was visible.
.PP
The probe that settled it was
.BR "yview" " with no argument,"
which answers the view as two fractions: it read
.B "0.5 0.5"
after a
.BR "yview moveto 0.5" ,
and top equal to bot means the visible-line count is zero \(em
.B "act.height/lineheight"
had truncated to nothing. The scroll had been working all along; there was
no row to put on screen.
.PP
.B "And \(lqdrew something\(rq hid it,"
because it counts bytes differing from white and the toplevel's grey
background satisfies that whatever the widget does. The four degenerate
cases reported 28736 of 28800 bytes of ink while rendering no listbox at
all.
.PP
.B "What catches this class is the differ-from-base rule, applied to EVERY"
variation rather than only to new ones. It had been applied to the cases
added last and not to the ones written first, which is exactly where the
degenerate widgets were. All twenty variations are now held to it, and the
figures moved accordingly:
.B "listbox with a selection"
went from covering nothing to differing from a plain listbox by 6888 bytes.
.PP
.B "Two toplevels drawing alternately"
are covered, and were the last mechanism gap.
.BR scene.c 's
walk is a single static struct shared by every toplevel, so interleaved
drawing is where its state could leak \(em a depth that does not return to
zero, or a scene pointer left at a tree the flush has freed. Each of two
toplevels is reconfigured and updated in turn, three times, and each image
must match the same widget rendered alone, through both paths. It does, at
0 bytes.
.PP
Two details make that check mean something. Bare
.B update
calls on a toplevel with nothing pending do nothing, so each round
reconfigures the text first to force a repaint; and the case reads
.B "render count"
of its own, because the global one is taken before it runs and the identity
would otherwise pass if both arms had somehow taken the direct path. It
builds 2 trees.
.PP
.I Multiple
widgets are not covered here and do not need to be:
.IR tkofftest (1)
compares the same two paths over a pane holding one of every widget kind, so
two trees built before either is flushed \(em the invariant
.IR libtk 's
walk exists to maintain \(em is exercised there, along with one comparison
per focusable widget, since only one widget can hold the keyboard and a
listbox draws neither its ring nor its active item's box while an entry has
it.
.PP
The division between the two is worth knowing. This program renders ONE
case into a FRESH image, so when it fails it names the widget and the state;
.IR tkofftest (1)
renders everything at once, so it covers interactions this cannot and tells
you less about which of them broke. A second
.I toplevel
is now covered here, and by nothing else.
.PP
The "drew something" check counts bytes differing from the white the image
was filled with, so a widget that painted only its own grey background
satisfies it \(em a posted menu reported the full 28800 bytes before and
after it was posted. It is a guard against comparing two blank images, not
evidence that a case's content was drawn.
.PP
Two things guard the content instead. Every setup command's reply is
checked, so a selection or an item that did not take is reported rather
than silently leaving a default widget to be compared with itself. And each
case that is a
.I variation
of another must be shown to DIFFER from the case it varies \(em the
non-default font from a plain label, the focused entry from a plain entry,
the posted menu from the same menu unposted \(em or it is reported as
.B skipped
rather than passed. That rule was added because a font that cannot be
opened, or a menu with no backing image, produces a case that passes while
covering nothing.
.PP
The two controls above rule out the node path not running and the
comparison not working. They do not amount to showing that a
.I deliberate
defect in a scene conversion would be caught; that needs the defect
injected, which cannot be done from here.
.PP
.B "Where it has actually been run."
The 22-case version of this program was run on a real Pi 3B and reported 46
ok, 0 fail, 1 skipped. That is the only
.I hardware
figure it has, and it does not generalise forward: the six cases added since
\(em a focused entry, a clipped label, non-default colours, a canvas fill by
name, a canvas polygon and a posted menu \(em and the
.B Maxwalk
nest case have run under
.IR qemu (1)
and on the host only. Quote "46/0/1, 22 cases, silicon" or "66/0/2, 28
cases, QEMU", never "verified on silicon" unqualified; a stale number with
correct provenance is still stale.
.PP
The nest case in particular does not need hardware and has deliberately not
had it:
.B Maxwalk
is a compile-time constant and TCG executes the same instructions, so a boot
would buy a figure that distinguishes nothing. What would be worth hardware
is a case whose behaviour could plausibly differ there \(em a cache-visibility
question, or anything touching the framebuffer rather than an allocated
image.
.PP
Nothing here looks at the screen, so it says nothing about what the board
displays \(em only about what the two paths compute. A readback cannot see
tearing or a stale buffer at all.
